fascination moderator and CBS News  senior foreign affairs correspondent  Margaret Brennan is here the former  Deputy Secretary of State under  President Obama William burns is with us  as well he also served as ambassador to  Russia from 2005 to 2008 and met  President Vladimir Putin while in that  role good morning to you both ambassador  burns let me start with you you said  that the the best thing that can happen  from this meeting is that it would be  Hippocratic which is to say the  Hippocratic oath first do no harm so  what would that look like  well I think first it means it's  essential that the president made clear  to President Putin that the election  meddling which was documented in the  indictments last week is a major assault  on our democracy and that it's very  serious business I think it means not  succumbing to the illusion that personal  rapport can transform relationships or  that there are grand bargains to be done  out there and third I think you can  focus on a few practical steps specially  in arms control where the new start  agreement on strategic nuclear weapons  expires in a couple of years and that's  a conversation we should resume with the  Russians that personal rapport point it  seems something that is that something  that every president kind of has to  learn I remember he previous in  administration's for example when George  W Bush came into office they said we're  not going to fall for just this cozy  relationship with a Russian leader there  are interests separate and apart from  these personal relationships well  personal relationships obviously matter  but they got to be tethered to history  to a sense of what it is that you want  to accomplish this isn't just about  getting along with another leader it's  about pursuing American interests and  that's what you got to keep them focused  resident Trump in the past has always  blamed his predecessors for the reason  that the relationship between Russia and  the u.s. is so bad is that a fair  criticism I don't think it is I mean in  the tweet the president sent out this  morning he was basically saying that not  only are relations at low ebb since the  end of the Cold War but it's basically  his predecessors fault and that also is  not tethered to history in the sense  that it ignores the fact that you know  of Russia's aggression in Ukraine in  2014 that's intervention in Syria in  2015 and it's interference in our  elections in 2016  Margaret the opening comments that we  just heard from the two presidents  President Trump said that he will ask  Vladimir Putin a lot of questions  aren't we past the part of asking  questions that happened at their last  meeting don't we need answers right now  in a response in terms of the election  mentally yes well I mean as the  president said what do you expect a  Perry Mason moment he seems to be sort  of dismissing this idea that a  confrontation is what is really needed  even though he prides himself on tough  talk the question I think is sort of he  wants to turn the page he wants  something he can put the Trump brand on  in terms of a new deal whether it's arms  control or whether it's some kind of  breakthrough in Syria or in Ukraine the  concern as diplomats that said to me  leading up to this is let's just have  our fingers crossed that NATO is good  enough and he doesn't give Putin  anything what does that mean they're  concerned perhaps could he call off  military exercises in the Baltic  something like what he did when he met  with the North Korean leader recently  calls something off without getting  anything in return from Vladimir Putin  could he do something like recognizes  annexation of Crimea which despite the  denials of US officials on the record  they will say off the record yes he has  opened the door to that and why does  that matter because you are then  blessing the seizure of territory by for  something that international law has you  know been established to try to prevent  those kind of things so I don't expect  the President to go in with a  confrontation on that but as major  Garrett pointed out the Trump  administration on paper does have a  tough policy a hard-line position  against Russia whether it's liquefied  national natural gas as he laid out  whether its arms to Ukraine it just  hasn't been backed up by what the  president actually says ambassador what  do you make of this the difference of  the styles between the two men  I think the the format of this summit  we're you know one on one meeting is  sort of a centerpiece of it you know  very loosely prepared summit is a big  contrast of summits I've known over the  last thirty years where there are  one-on-one meetings but they're a small  part of a very carefully planned sum at  first second you know you have a  president President Trump who prides  himself on winning  operating from his guys I don't think  flirting where Putin never wings yes you  know he  he has his own well practiced narrative  about what's happened over the last 20  almost 20 years since he's been  president he's deeply experienced and so  he's going to try to take advantage of  that and take advantage of his command  of detail and he has an advantage I  think he does in the sense that he's a  vastly experienced leader I think he's  not at all sentimental about personal  rapport he can charm as well as bully  and I think you'll see a lot of charm  from him and the one-on-one meeting with  President Roh and you have professionals  like the Ambassador to protect a  president even though he says he doesn't  want to trust the bureaucracy under  normal circumstances but not here thanks  to both Margaret and ambassador burns
 

